<template name="window_shell_bg" content="content">
<head></head>
<!-- Modern theme's background-only window shell -- a sibling of window_shell.rml (see its own
     comment for the general family) with no header block and no data-model of any kind. This
     variant exists specifically for background-context companion documents (the *_bg.rml pattern,
     see npc_shop_bg.rml for the mechanism) -- those are deliberately 100% static markup (no
     RmlModelBinder), which window_shell's own has_title/title bindings would have nothing to bind
     against. CGenericConfirmDialog's own generic_confirm_dialog_bg.rml is the first consumer; its
     real header lives in the paired foreground document instead.

     Deliberately empty head (Template::Load() requires this element to exist at all, but nothing
     needs linking here) -- unlike window_shell.rml (whose own paired stylesheet has no token(...)
     calls at all), the classes used below (.modern-shell-edge/.modern-groove) live in base.rcss,
     which is full of token(...) markers. RmlTheme.cpp's own InlineTokenizedStylesheet() substitutes
     token(...) markers by scanning the raw text of whatever RML file
     UI::RmlBridge::LoadThemedDocument()/CreateBackgroundDocument() was asked to load, rewriting
     each of ITS OWN direct rcss link entries in place -- a template file's own head is never one of
     those, since RmlUi's own TemplateCache::LoadTemplate() reads a template file directly from
     disk, entirely bypassing this project's C++ loading path. An rcss link to base.rcss living here
     instead would reach RmlUi as raw, unsubstituted text, and every token(...) call in it would
     silently fail to parse. The consuming document links base.rcss itself, directly in its own
     head, the normal way (see generic_confirm_dialog_bg.rml).

     Deliberately does NOT apply .modern-frame to #panel -- unlike window_shell's own metal-rail
     look, this consumer's frame paint is its own bespoke hatched-iron treatment (matches the
     project's own reference mockup for plain confirm/message dialogs), not the standard bevel-
     border frame. Only .modern-shell-edge/.modern-groove are shared here (their own box-shadow
     highlight alphas are close enough that a same-document override in the consumer's own rcss can
     restore the exact original values, see generic_confirm_dialog_bg.rcss); #panel's own
     background/border/decorator and #content's own fill paint stay 100% the consuming document's
     responsibility, same as .modern-content-well's own "position and paint are the consumer's job"
     convention already establishes.

     A consumer links this file as a template resource in its own head section and sets this
     template's name as its own body element's template attribute (see
     generic_confirm_dialog_bg.rml for the worked example), with no children of its own needed --
     nothing gets spliced into #content below for a purely static bg document, but the slot exists
     for a future consumer that does need one. As with window_shell.rml, avoid a literal
     tag-shaped example anywhere in this file's own comments, and never join the words head
     and body with a bare slash, anywhere in this file, comments included (RmlUi's
     Template::Load()/FindTag() text search is comment-blind). -->
<body id="panel">
	<div class="modern-shell-edge"></div>
	<div class="modern-groove"></div>
	<div id="content"></div>
</body>
</template>
